course: dictionary
lesson: 13983
title: Lodging: a place to stay
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lodging is a noun. It means a place to stay for a short time, such as a room in someone's house or in an inn. «The travellers found lodging at a small inn by the river.» The travellers needed somewhere to sleep, and the inn gave them a room.
explain: In the UK, you will often see lodging in the plural, lodgings. Lodgings are a rented room or rooms in a house. «Ruby rented lodgings above the bakery.» Ruby pays money to live in those rooms, but they are not her own house.
explain: Lodging can also be a cost in a maths problem. «Their lodging cost ten pounds a night for three nights.» Multiply the price by the number of nights: 10 × 3 = 30, so the lodging cost thirty pounds in total.
explain: Lodging is also the -ing form of the verb lodge. It can mean staying somewhere as a paying guest, or getting stuck somewhere. «A fish bone was lodging in his throat.» Here the bone is stuck, so lodging means stuck.
fact: Lodging means a place to stay for a short time.
fact: Lodgings is often used in the plural in the UK, and it means a rented room or rooms in a house.
fact: «Board and lodging» means meals and a room to stay in.
fact: A «lodging house» is a house where people pay to stay.
fact: When lodging is used in a maths problem, it is a cost you work out by multiplying the price by the number of nights.
exercise: «The travellers needed lodging for the night.» What does lodging mean here? | A long journey | A kind of boat | *A place to stay | A type of food
exercise: «Their lodging cost ten pounds a night.» In a maths problem, what does lodging usually mean? | The name of a street | *The price of a room for the nights | The number of people in the room | The distance to the inn
exercise: «The fish bone was lodging in his throat.» What does lodging mean here? | Singing loudly | *Stuck somewhere | Growing very fast | Swimming away
test: «Board and lodging» means what? | Only a bed | Clean clothes | *Meals and a room | Money to spend
test: In the UK, what are lodgings? | *A rented room or rooms in a house | A ship's deck | A kind of cake | A mountain path
exam: «They paid for lodging at the inn.» What does lodging mean here? | A long walk | *A place to stay for a short time | A lost purse | A loud noise
